WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.204
The simplest way to organize key plus page number pairs is a hash index.

00:00:05.204 --> 00:00:08.409
Hash the key, get the bucket, jump straight to that disk location.

00:00:08.409 --> 00:00:11.613
Constant time on the lookup.

00:00:11.613 --> 00:00:14.817
The catch is one line.

00:00:14.817 --> 00:00:18.022
WHERE user id equals forty-two is fast.

00:00:18.022 --> 00:00:21.226
A range gets no help at all.

00:00:21.226 --> 00:00:24.168
Here is the whole thing on screen.

00:00:24.168 --> 00:00:27.110
On the left, the data pages that hold the actual rows.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:30.052
On the right, the hash index pages.

00:00:30.052 --> 00:00:32.993
A hash index stores the same key plus page number pairs that any index does.

00:00:32.993 --> 00:00:35.935
The hash function picks which index page each pair lands in.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.877
A lookup does three things.

00:00:38.877 --> 00:00:41.819
Hash the search key in memory.

00:00:41.819 --> 00:00:44.761
Reed the right index page off disk.

00:00:44.761 --> 00:00:47.703
Follow the pointer to the data page.

00:00:47.703 --> 00:00:50.645
Two disk reeds, regardless of table size.

00:00:50.645 --> 00:00:53.587
Walk two cases.

00:00:53.587 --> 00:00:56.528
First, Alice.

00:00:56.528 --> 00:00:59.470
Hash of Alice lands in index page zero.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:02.412
The entry says Alice points to page two.

00:01:02.412 --> 00:01:05.354
Reed page two.

00:01:05.354 --> 00:01:08.296
Two I Os, done.

00:01:08.296 --> 00:01:11.238
Now Dan.

00:01:11.238 --> 00:01:14.180
Dan appears in three different data pages, zero, one, and four, because duplicate keys fan out.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.122
Hash of Dan lands in index page one, and that entry points to all three.

00:01:17.122 --> 00:01:20.063
Reed all three.

00:01:20.063 --> 00:01:23.005
Four I Os.

00:01:23.005 --> 00:01:25.947
The cost grows with duplicates of the search key, never with the size of the table.

00:01:25.947 --> 00:01:28.889
That is what constant time buys you.

00:01:28.889 --> 00:01:32.288
Two ways to organize the index itself.

00:01:32.288 --> 00:01:35.688
The basic version is one giant dictionary.

00:01:35.688 --> 00:01:39.087
Alice points to page two.

00:01:39.087 --> 00:01:42.487
Dan points to a list of pages.

00:01:42.487 --> 00:01:45.886
Simple, and it works until the dictionary stops fitting in RAM.

00:01:45.886 --> 00:01:49.285
One billion entries at sixteen bytes each is sixteen gigabytes.

00:01:49.285 --> 00:01:52.685
At that point every lookup starts with a disk reed.

00:01:52.685 --> 00:01:56.084
The fix is partitioning.

00:01:56.084 --> 00:01:59.484
Split the index across N pages, each one around sixty-four megabytes, each fitting comfortably.

00:01:59.484 --> 00:02:02.883
The extra hash step is free, and the semantics are unchanged.

00:02:02.883 --> 00:02:06.141
What hash indexes win on.

00:02:06.141 --> 00:02:09.400
WHERE user id equals forty-two.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.659
WHERE order id equals ABC dash one two three.

00:02:12.659 --> 00:02:15.917
Any predicate that resolves to a single key value.

00:02:15.917 --> 00:02:19.175
Equality, primary key, session id, cache key.

00:02:19.175 --> 00:02:22.434
What they break on.

00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:25.692
WHERE age greater than twenty-one.

00:02:25.692 --> 00:02:28.951
ORDER BY created at.

00:02:28.951 --> 00:02:32.209
WHERE name like A percent.

00:02:32.209 --> 00:02:35.468
Any predicate that spans a range, needs sorted order, or matches a prefix.

00:02:35.468 --> 00:02:38.726
The hash scatters neighbouring keys on purpose, so sorted access is impossible.

00:02:38.726 --> 00:02:41.985
For that, reach for a B-plus tree.

00:02:41.985 --> 00:02:46.223
The algorithm in two halves.

00:02:46.223 --> 00:02:50.460
Build.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:54.698
Pick a bucket count, often a prime.

00:02:54.698 --> 00:02:58.936
For each tuple, hash the key, mod by the bucket count, append the entry to that bucket's list.

00:02:58.936 --> 00:03:03.173
Lookup.

00:03:03.173 --> 00:03:07.411
Hash the search key, mod by the bucket count, scan that one bucket for a match, return the pointer.

00:03:07.411 --> 00:03:11.648
Average case is constant time.

00:03:11.648 --> 00:03:15.886
A poor hash clusters keys into a few buckets and degrades lookup to linear, so production databases use well-studied hashes like MurmurHash and xxHash.

00:03:15.886 --> 00:03:19.628
Constant time equality lookups.

00:03:19.628 --> 00:03:23.369
Two disk reeds for a unique key, a few more for duplicates.

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:27.111
Hard to beat for point queries, and no help for ranges or sorts.

00:03:27.111 --> 00:03:30.853
Neighbouring keys scatter to random buckets on purpose.

00:03:30.853 --> 00:03:34.595
Partitioning keeps a huge index practical, each partition still fitting in RAM.

00:03:34.595 --> 00:03:38.336
Next, the index that handles both point lookups and range scans with one shape.

00:03:38.336 --> 00:03:42.078
B-plus trees.
